Marionette AirDrop driver candidate 0039 -- transmit parameters for the
tx-sniffer exception contract.

Applies on top of 0038 (native/airdrop/driver/0038-monitor-exception-frame-contract.patch).
File: qca-wifi-host-cmn/dp/wifi3.0/dp_tx.c

Why
---
0038 took the tx_sniffer contract exactly as OCB and the mcopy tx-sniffer
path use it -- SEND_AS_STANDALONE plus a host opaque cookie and nothing
else -- and the firmware answered with the very same assert it had already
given the plain vdev-based frame in 0037:

    mhi mhi_1107_00.01.00: [E][mhi_process_sfr] cmnos_thread_patch.c:1170:0x8
    Asserted in:0x18dc754:0x1801164, line#2834

The same SFR string twice in a row from two different host paths says the
target is not objecting to which host path the frame takes, it is objecting
to the frame itself.  A frame that reaches the target as a TQM-bypass MSDU
still has to become a PPDU, and on a monitor vdev there is no rate control,
no peer and no tx-spec to fall back on, so an all-zero ext2 header leaves
the firmware with nothing to transmit.

What
----
Fill the HTT MSDU extension header the way the mesh exception path does
(dp_tx_extract_mesh_meta_data()), which is the only in-tree producer of
host-built exception frames carrying their own transmit parameters:

    host_tx_desc_pool  1   mandatory: the header states that whenever
                          firmware uses TQM-BYPASS for a data TID the host
                          must set it, and dp_tx_prepare_desc_single()
                          routes every exception frame through that WAR.
    update_peer_cache   1   keep these values for the following packets.
    learning_frame     0    NOT set on purpose: it tells firmware to drop
                          the frame instead of enqueueing it to TQM, which
                          is the opposite of injection.  Mesh clears it for
                          unencrypted frames too.

    valid_pwr / valid_mcs_mask / valid_nss_mask / valid_preamble_type /
    valid_retries / valid_bw_info / valid_encrypt_type   1

    pream_type  OFDM, mcs 0 (6 Mb/s), nss 1, 20 MHz, 10 dBm, 3 retries,
    encrypt_type 0 (NO_ENCRYPT).

A fixed 20 MHz OFDM 6 Mb/s is deliberate: it is the most robust rate the
radio offers on both AWDL social channels (6 and 44/149) and does not
depend on the peer supporting the monitor vdev phy mode.  10 dBm is enough
for a peer a metre away and keeps the frame inside the power limits of
those channels.  chanfreq stays 0, which means "use the vdev home channel",
i.e. the channel the monitor vdev was started on.

Result on device
----------------
See native/airdrop/driver/ROADMAP.md section 6 and build.json.

@@ -2794,6 +2794,58 @@
 
 	qdf_mem_zero(meta_data, sizeof(struct htt_tx_msdu_desc_ext2_t));
 
+	/* Marionette (0039): specify the transmit parameters, do not leave them
+	 * to the vdev.  Candidate 0038 took the tx_sniffer contract exactly as
+	 * OCB and the mcopy tx-sniffer path use it -- SEND_AS_STANDALONE plus a
+	 * host opaque cookie and nothing else -- and the firmware answered with
+	 * the same assert it gave the plain vdev-based frame in 0037
+	 * (mhi_process_sfr: cmnos_thread_patch.c:1170 ... line#2834, i.e. an
+	 * identical RDDM twice in a row).  A frame that reaches the target as a
+	 * TQM-bypass MSDU still has to become a PPDU, and on a monitor vdev
+	 * there is no rate control, no peer and no tx-spec to fall back on, so
+	 * an empty ext2 header gives the firmware nothing to transmit.
+	 * dp_tx_prepare_htt_metadata() already prepends this header when
+	 * is_tx_sniffer is set; what follows fills it in the way the mesh
+	 * exception path does (dp_tx_extract_mesh_meta_data()), which is the
+	 * only in-tree producer of host-built exception frames that carry their
+	 * own rate, power, bandwidth and retry information.
+	 *
+	 * host_tx_desc_pool is not optional here: the header states that
+	 * whenever firmware uses TQM-BYPASS for a data TID, the host must set
+	 * it, and dp_tx_prepare_desc_single() routes every exception frame
+	 * through the bypass WAR out of this same call.
+	 *
+	 * update_peer_cache asks the firmware to remember these values for the
+	 * following packets.  learning_frame is deliberately NOT set: it tells
+	 * firmware to drop the frame instead of enqueueing it to TQM, which is
+	 * the opposite of injection (mesh clears it for unencrypted frames).
+	 *
+	 * A fixed 20 MHz OFDM 6 Mb/s (mcs 0, one NSS, OFDM preamble) is used on
+	 * purpose: it is the most robust rate the radio offers on both the
+	 * 2.4 GHz and the 5 GHz AWDL social channels, and a low rate never
+	 * depends on the receiver supporting the vdev phy mode.  10 dBm is
+	 * enough for a peer a metre away and keeps the frame well inside the
+	 * power limits of the AWDL channels. */
+	meta_data->host_tx_desc_pool = 1;
+	meta_data->update_peer_cache = 1;
+
+	meta_data->valid_pwr = 1;
+	meta_data->valid_mcs_mask = 1;
+	meta_data->valid_nss_mask = 1;
+	meta_data->valid_preamble_type = 1;
+	meta_data->valid_retries = 1;
+	meta_data->valid_bw_info = 1;
+	meta_data->valid_encrypt_type = 1;
+
+	meta_data->pream_type = HTT_TX_MSDU_EXT2_DESC_PREAM_OFDM;
+	meta_data->mcs_mask = 1 << 0;
+	meta_data->nss_mask = 1;
+	meta_data->power = 20;
+	meta_data->retry_limit = 3;
+	meta_data->encrypt_type = 0;
+	meta_data->dyn_bw = 0;
+	meta_data->bw_mask = HTT_TX_MSDU_EXT2_DESC_BW_20MHZ_M;
+
 	HTT_TX_MSDU_EXT2_DESC_FLAG_SEND_AS_STANDALONE_SET
 				(msdu_info->meta_data[5], 1);
 	HTT_TX_MSDU_EXT2_DESC_FLAG_HOST_OPAQUE_VALID_SET
